Supabase Root 2021 CA — pinned for strict TLS verification (Housekeeping #33)

This is the public root certificate for Supabase's own private CA
(subject/issuer: C=US, ST=Delware, L=New Castle, O=Supabase Inc,
CN=Supabase Root 2021 CA; valid 2021-04-28 to 2031-04-26), which their
Postgres connection pooler's certificate chain is signed by. It is NOT
in the public trust stores Node checks by default, which is why
`rejectUnauthorized: true` fails with "self-signed certificate in
certificate chain" unless this file is explicitly supplied as `ca`.

Fetched and verified directly against the live pooler, 2026-08-23:
  openssl s_client -starttls postgres \
    -connect aws-1-ap-south-1.pooler.supabase.com:6543 -showcerts

Re-verify against that command (or the current POSTGRES_URL host) if
Supabase ever rotates this root — a connection failure after rotation
is the safe, loud failure mode this pinning is meant to produce.

